  "body": "  Let be a continuous function (that is, one with no jumps or holes in the graph) and suppose that a graph of is given by the graph on the right in Figure .   Axes for plotting and, at right, the graph of .       Observe that for every value of that satisfies , the value of is constant. What does this tell you about the behavior of the graph of on this interval?    On what intervals other than do you expect to be a linear function? Why?    At which values of is not defined? What behavior does this lead you to expect to see in the graph of ?    Suppose that . On the axes provided at left in Figure , sketch an accurate graph of .           is linear.    On , and .    At ; must have sharp corners at these points.               Since is constant (with value ) on the interval , it follows that is linear on that same interval, since is increasing at a constant rate.    On , we also expect to be linear with slope , while on and , will be linear with slope ; in each case this is true because the value of is constant with the noted value on the interval.    From the given graph of , we observe that is undefined at . Since we have been given that is a continuous function, we can conclude that must have sharp corners on its graph at these points. Moreover, that makes sense in light of our earlier observations that show has constant slope on the intervals that connect at and the graph jumps from having slope to , and then back, and so on.           "
