  "body": "  For a certain function , its derivative is given by the function pictured in Figure .   The graph of .       What is the approximate slope of the tangent line to at the point ?    How many real number solutions can there be to the equation ? Justify your conclusion fully and carefully by explaining what you know about how the graph of must behave based on the given graph of .    On the interval , how many times does the concavity of change? Why?    Use the provided graph to estimate the value of .           .    At most one.     .     .          From the given graph of , we may estimate that .    Observe that is always positive. This tells us that must be always increasing. Therefore, it follows that may cross the -axis at most one time, and hence there can be at most one solution to .    On , changes concavity times, since changes from increasing to decreasing or from decreasing to increasing times. Whenever is increasing, is positive, and whenever is decreasing, is negative. Hence, whenever changes from increasing to decreasing or vice versa, it follows that changes sign, and this causes a change in the concavity of . We therefore see that while is always increasing, changes concavity many times.    From the given graph of , we can see that and . Using a central difference, it follows that .      "
