  "title": "",
  "body": "  For a certain function , its derivative is known to be . Note that you do not know a formula for .   At what -value(s) is ? Justify your answer algebraically, but include a graph of to support your conclusion.    Reasoning graphically, for what intervals of -values is ? What does this tell you about the behavior of the original function ? Explain.    Assuming that , estimate the value of by finding and using the tangent line approximation to at . Is your estimate larger or smaller than the true value of ? Justify your answer.           .    On ; is concave up.     , and this estimate is larger than the true value of .          Since raised to any power is always positive (and thus never ), it follows that the only value that makes is . This is confirmed by plotting where we only see the graph cross the -axis at (though we do see the graph approach the -axis) as increases or decreases without bound).    We know that wherever is increasing. From the graph of , we see that is increasing approximately on the interval , and thus is concave up on this interval.    The tangent line approximation at is . Thus, . Since at and around , is concave down there, and therefore the tangent line lies above the curve. This makes our estimate larger than the true value of .      "
