  "body": "  A baseball diamond is a square with sides feet long. Suppose a baseball player is advancing from second to third base at the rate of feet per second, and an umpire is standing on home plate. Let be the angle between the third baseline and the line of sight from the umpire to the runner. How fast is changing when the runner is feet from third base?     radians per second.    Let represent the distance from the runner to third base along the path from second to third, let be the distance from the umpire at home plate to the runner, and call the angle between the third baseline and the line of sight from the umpire to the runner. See the figure below.     Each of , , and is implicitly a function of . We are given that the runner is advancing at a rate of feet per second, so . Since we want to know , we want to relate and .  We first observe that by definition, . Having related and , we can differentiate both sides of the preceding equation with respect to . By the chain rule, , and thus At the instant , since , . Hence, at this same instance, . Recall we also know that . Applying this information to the equation that relates the rates of change of and , radians per second.   "
