  "title": "",
  "body": "  Based on the given information about each function, decide whether the function has global maximum, a global minimum, neither, both, or that it is not possible to say without more information. Assume that each function is twice differentiable and defined for all real numbers, unless noted otherwise. In each case, write one sentence to explain your conclusion.    is a function such that for every .     is a function with two critical numbers and (where ), and for , for , and for .     is a function with two critical numbers and (where ), and for , for , and for . In addition, and .     is a function differentiable everywhere except at and for and for .          Not enough information is given.    Global minimum at .    Global minimum at ; global maximum at .    Not enough information is provided.          It's not possible to say without more information: for instance, the functions , , and are each always concave up, but only one of them has a global minimum. We can say for sure that by being always concave up, cannot have a global maximum, and it also cannot have both a global maximum and a global minimum. It might have a global minimum, or it might have neither.    By being twice differentiable, we know that any time is a critical number of . Thus, given the signs of on the stated intervals, is decreasing before and decreasing on , with a horizontal tangent line at . In addition, is increasing for . Thus, we know that has a global minimum at .    Since is always decreasing for , always increasing for , and always decreasing for , we know that has a local minimum at and a local maximum at . Moreover, these local extremes are both global extremes since as . In particular, since decreases to for and increases on , it follows that is the largest value of on , and that . Similarly, since decreases on and approaches as , and increases on , it must be the case that is the least value of on and that . Hence, is always negative on and is always positive on . From all of these observations, it follows that has a global minimum at and a global maximum at .    First, observe that is defined for all real numbers by assumption, so has some finite value. However, is not differentiable at and is concave up to the left of and concave down to the right of . This indicates that has a relative maximum at . Not enough information is provided, however, to determine if this relative maximum is a global maximum, nor if happens to have a global minimum at some other point.      "
