  "body": "  The rate at which pollution escapes a scrubbing process at a manufacturing plant increases over time as filters and other technologies become less effective. For this particular example, assume that the rate of pollution (in tons per week) is given by the function that is pictured in Figure .      Use the graph to estimate the value of on the interval .    What is the meaning of in terms of the pollution discharged by the plant?    Suppose that . Use this formula for to compute on .    Determine an upper bound on the total amount of pollution that can escape the plant during the pictured four week time period that is accurate within an error of at most one ton of pollution.      The rate, , of pollution in tons per week.           .       The total tonnage of pollution escaping the scrubbing process in the time interval weeks.     .        tons.          We estimate the value of at the midpoint of each subinterval and then multiply these values by the width ( ) of each subinterval to estimate the areas of the rectangles shown in the following figure. We use the following approximations: , , , , so . \\[\\]       Since is measured in tons\/week and in weeks, the units of are tons. So approximates the total tonnage of pollution escaping the scrubbing process in the time interval weeks.    Now we are using subintervals, so . We evaluate at the left endpoint of each subinterval and multiply by to determine the areas of the rectangles shown in the following figure. So .       Given that is an increasing function, a right sum will always give us an overestimate of the total amount of pollution (in tons) on the interval . We use computational technology to compute a few right sums: , , , . These sums don't seem to change in the tens place as we make sufficiently large, so an upper bound on the total amount of pollution on the interval that is accurate to within one ton is tons.      "
