  "body": "  Suppose that the velocity of a moving object is given by , measured in feet per second, and that this function is valid for .   Write an expression involving definite integrals whose value is the total change in position of the object on the interval .    Use appropriate technology (such as an applet ) to compute Riemann sums to estimate the object's total change in position on . Work to ensure that your estimate is accurate to two decimal places, and explain how you know this to be the case.    Write an expression involving definite integrals whose value is the total distance traveled by the object on .    Use appropriate technology to compute Riemann sums to estimate the object's total distance travelled on . Work to ensure that your estimate is accurate to two decimal places, and explain how you know this to be the case.    What is the object's average velocity on , accurate to two decimal places?          The total change in position, , is .     .    The total distance traveled, , is .     .     feet per second.          We observe that at , , and , and is positive on the intervals and , while is negative on . The total change in position, , of the object is thus given by .    Using computational technology, we use the midpoint rule to estimate the total change of position, . We find that , , , and . Since it appears that neither of the first two decimal places are changing as we increase the number of subintervals, it looks like is accurate to two decimal places.    We noted in (a) where is positive and negative. In particular, on , , and thus will give us a negative change in position; the distance traveled on this interval is therefore , and hence the total distance traveled, , on is .    To use Riemann sums to estimate , we have to estimate each of the three integrals individually. We find that    ,     , and        From experimenting with different -values in , it is apparent that each of these individual estimates is accurate to at least decimal places, and thus we expect their sum to be accurate to at least . Hence, . Therefore is an estimate of distance traveled accurate to at least decimal places.    We know that the average velocity on is given by the integral expression . We estimated the definite integral in (b), so using that result, feet per second is the object's average velocity, accurate to at least two decimal places. It makes sense that the average velocity is positive since the total change in position is positive.      "
