  "title": "",
  "body": "  Consider the curve and the portion of its graph that lies in the first quadrant between the -axis and the first positive value of for which . Let denote the region bounded by this portion of , the -axis, and the -axis.   Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact arc length of that lies along the upper boundary of . Use technology appropriately to evaluate the integral you find.    Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact area of . Use technology appropriately to evaluate the integral you find.    Suppose that the region is revolved around the -axis. Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact volume of the solid of revolution that is generated. Use technology appropriately to evaluate the integral you find.    Suppose instead that is revolved around the -axis. If possible, set up an integral expression whose value is the exact volume of the solid of revolution and evaluate the integral using appropriate technology. If not possible, explain why.           .     .     .     .          Plotting the function and using technology to find where intersects the -axis, we see that the region as pictured in the following figure.     To apply the formula we developed for arc length, we need the derivative of . By the chain rule, , so the arc length of that lies along the upper boundary of is . Using technology to evaluate the integral, we find .    The area of is .    Revolving around the -axis, we can use the disk method with slices perpendicular to the -axis to find the volume of the resulting region. Noting that the radius of each such disk is and the width of each such disk is , we know the volume of each slice is and thus the total volume is     If we instead revolve around the -axis, we need to use horizontal disks of thickness in order to apply the disk method. To do that, we also need to express as a function of , rather than as a function of as given. Thus we take the given function and solve the equation for . First, we observe and thus using the inverse cosine, and therefore so that . Now, a typical slice has radius with thickness , and volume . Integrating to add up the slices from to , we have the volume of the solid of revolution is .      "
