  "body": "  Consider the curves given by and . For each of the following problems, you should include a sketch of the region\/solid being considered, as well as a labeled representative slice.   Sketch the region bounded by the -axis and the curves and up to the first positive value of at which they intersect. What is the exact intersection point of the curves?    Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact area of .    Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact volume of the solid of revolution generated by revolving about the -axis.    Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact volume of the solid of revolution generated by revolving about the -axis.    Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact volume of the solid of revolution generated by revolving about the line .    Set up a definite integral whose value is the exact volume of the solid of revolution generated by revolving about the line .                .     .          .                  See the sketch above. The exact intersection point of the curves is where in the first quadrant, and thus at the point .    The area of is given by the definite integral .    Revolving about the -axis and using slices perpendicular to the axis with thickness , the outer radius of the resulting washers is , while the inner radius of the washers is . The volume of a typical slice is and thus using an integral to sum the slices over the interval to , we find the volume of the solid is .    Revolving about the -axis, the resulting figure has solid cross sections that are disks perpendicular to the -axis with thickness , but the radius that governs the radius of each disk changes at the height where in the interval from to . We also need to express the two curves that bound the region with as a function of . On the interval from to , the sine function provides the boundary, which we write as ; on the interval from to , the cosine function provides the boundary, which we write as . On the lower interval, a typical slice has volume . Using similar reasoning for the upper interval and summing the slices using appropriate definite integrals, we find that     Draw a picture. Revolving about the line , we see that cross-sections perpendicular to the line are washers with thickness , outer radius , and inner radius , since on the interval . Thus, the volume of a typical slice is and hence integrating to sum the total volume, we find .    Revolving about the line , the resulting figure has solid cross sections that are washers perpendicular to the -axis with thickness ; the inner radius is always , but the outer radius that governs the larger radius of each washer changes at the height where in the interval from to . As in (d), we express the two curves that bound the region with as a function of . On the interval from to , ; on the interval from to , . On the lower interval, a typical slice has volume . Using similar reasoning for the upper interval and summing the slices using appropriate definite integrals, we find that       "
