  "title": "",
  "body": "  Consider the finite region that is bounded by the curves , , and .   Determine a definite integral whose value is the area of the region enclosed by the two curves.    Find an expression involving one or more definite integrals whose value is the volume of the solid of revolution generated by revolving the region about the line .    Determine an expression involving one or more definite integrals whose value is the volume of the solid of revolution generated by revolving the region about the -axis.    Find an expression involving one or more definite integrals whose value is the perimeter of the region .           .          .     .             A picture of the region is shown in the figure above. The point of intersection occurs when , so , which implies and thus so . Since on the interval , the area of the region between the two curves is .    (You should draw a picture of the solid, based on the figure above, with the region revolved about .) We slice vertically so that our cross sections are washers with thickness . Because we are revolving about , the outer radius is , while the inner radius is . Hence the volume of a typical slice, which has thickness is given by . Hence, the volume of the solid obtained by revolving the region around the line is given by     (Draw a picture of the solid, based on revolving the region about the -axis.) We slice horizontally and thus the resulting cross sections are cylinders with thickness . The radius of the disks changes as passes through , so we need two different integrals to find the total volume. Since we are slicing horizontally, we will integrate with respect to , so we need to solve both of our equations for as functions of . Observe that implies , so and thus , and similarly implies . The portion of the curve defined by that determines one of the boundaries is the left hand portion of the parabola, so , while the portion of the curve that determines the other boundary is the right hand portion of the parabola, so . These two curves define the radius of the disk (one for , the other for ) and the lowest point on the region is while the highest is . So a sum of integrals that represents the volume of the solid obtained by revolving the region around the -axis is     Recall that the length of a curve defined by a function on an interval is given by . To find the perimeter of the region we calculate the length of the curves defined by (for which ) and (for which ) on the interval and the length of the segment along as goes from to . This last length is , so the perimeter of the region is given by .      "
