  "body": "  Consider the curve and the portion of its graph that lies in the first quadrant between the -axis and the first positive value of for which . Let denote the region bounded by this portion of , the -axis, and the -axis. Assume that and are each measured in feet.   Picture the coordinate axes rotated degrees clockwise so that the positive -axis points straight down, and the positive -axis points to the right. Suppose that is rotated about the axis to form a solid of revolution, and we consider this solid as a storage tank. Suppose that the resulting tank is filled to a depth of feet with water weighing pounds per cubic foot. Find the amount of work required to lower the water in the tank until it is feet deep, by pumping the water to the top of the tank.    Again picture the coordinate axes rotated 90 degrees clockwise so that the positive -axis points straight down, and the positive -axis points to the right. Suppose that , together with its reflection across the -axis, forms one end of a storage tank that is 10 feet long. Suppose that the resulting tank is filled completely with water weighing pounds per cubic foot. Find a formula for a function that tells the amount of work required to lower the water by feet.    Suppose that the tank described in (b) is completely filled with water. Find the total force due to hydrostatic pressure exerted by the water on one end of the tank.              foot-pounds.     .     pounds.          First, we construct a picture of the tank as described. Below we see a sketch of in the setting where the positive -axis points down; note that this function first crosses the -axis at about . We reflect the curve from the first quadrant across the positive -axis to generate a solid of revolution. Cross-sections perpendicular to the -axis have thickness and are circular disks.     We now suppose that the resulting tank is filled to a depth of feet with water weighing pounds per cubic foot. Since the bottom of the tank is at , being full to a depth of feet means that there is water from to . To find the work done to pump the water until feet remain in the tank (i.e. to a level where ), we have to first find the weight of a typical slice, which requires us to know the volume of such a slice. Observe that for a slice at depth , . Since water weighs pounds per cubic foot, the weight (and thus force to be exerted) of such a slice is . Finally, the work done to pump such a slice to the top of the tank requires the slice to move units, and thus work for that slice is . To find the total work accomplished, we use a definite integral to sum the work done on each slice from (the top of the water when the tank is initially full to feet) to (the height of the water after foot has been emptied). Thus, . Evaluating the integral, we find foot-pounds.    If instead we envision the tank not as a solid of revolution but as a trough, where the two-dimensional figure above forms one end of a tank that is 10 feet long, we now have a similar problem but with slices that are thin rectangular slabs, rather than thin cylindrical disks. Note that each such slice is wide, feet long, and thick. Thus, for a slice at depth , . Since water weighs pounds per cubic foot, the weight (and thus force to be exerted) of such a slice is . Finally, the work done to pump such a slice to the top of the tank requires the slice to move units, and thus work for that slice is . Assuming that the tank is completely full, to lower the tank by feet, we consider slices from to , and thus using a definite integral to sum the work required for each slice, we find that the total work is .    For the trough described in (b), we now seek to find the total hydrostatic force on one of the ends of the tank when it is completely full. First, we note that a typical slice at location will be at a depth of feet below the water and have area . At any point on this slice, which again lies at depth , the pressure exerted is since pressure is the weight density of water times depth. Finally, since , the total force water is exerting on this slice is . Integrating to sum the total force, we find that pounds.      "
