  "body": "  Suppose that the population of a particular species is described by the function , where is expressed in millions. Suppose further that the population's rate of change is governed by the differential equation where is the function graphed below.        For which values of the population does the population increase?    For which values of the population does the population decrease?    If , how will the population change in time?    If the initial population satisfies , what will happen to the population after a very long time?    If the initial population satisfies , what will happen to the population after a very long time?    If the initial population satisfies , what will happen to the population after a very long time?    This model for a population's growth is sometimes called growth with a threshold. Explain why this is an appropriate name.           .     and .     will not change at all.    The population will decrease toward with always being positive.    The population will increase toward with always being between and .    The population will decrease toward with always being above .    There's a maximum threshold of .           increases for since for these -values.     decreases for and since for these -values.    If , , so will not change at all with time since its derivative is .    If the initial population satisfies , we expect that the population will decrease toward with always being positive. Since is always negative for , is always decreasing on this interval. The population can't drop below because at that value of , and at that value the population wouldn't change.    If the initial population satisfies , we expect that the population will increase toward with always being between and . Since is always positive for , is always increasing on this interval. The population can't increase above because at that value of , and at that value the population wouldn't change.    If the initial population satisfies , we expect that the population will decrease toward with always being above . Since is always negative for , is always decreasing on this interval. The population can't drop below because at that value of , and at that value the population wouldn't change.    It looks like there are two thresholds for the population: there's a minimum threshold of , since below that population, the population decreases to , and there's a maximum threshold of , where if we are above the minimum but below the maximum, the population works to increase toward the maximum threshold, while if the population is above the maximum, its value will fall back toward it.      "
