  "body": "  Consider the differential equation .      Sketch a slope field on the axes at right.    Sketch the solutions whose initial values are .    What do your sketches suggest is the solution whose initial value is ? Verify that this is indeed the solution to this initial value problem.    By considering the differential equation and the graphs you have sketched, what is the relationship between and at a point where a solution has a local minimum?                 Sketch curves through appropriate points in the slope field above.     .     and are equal.          Sketching the slopes at individual points by computing at various values of , we see the following slope field.       By following the slope field so that we pass through the points , , , , we see the collection of curves in the figure above.    It looks like the solution that passes through is a straight line with slope , and thus has the formula . To verify this function is a solution, we compute both and . Doing so, we see and . Thus, is indeed a solution to the differential equation.    From both the differential equation and the graphs we have sketched, it appears that and are equal at any point where a solution has a local minimum. This is because when , , and having the derivative be zero is required for any local minimum on a differentiable function.      "
