  "body": "  The population of a species of fish in a lake is where is measured in thousands of fish and is measured in months. The growth of the population is described by the differential equation .   Sketch a graph of and use it to determine the equilibrium solutions and whether they are stable or unstable. Write a complete sentence that describes the long-term behavior of the fish population.    Suppose now that the owners of the lake allow fishers to remove 1000 fish from the lake every month (remember that is measured in thousands of fish). Modify the differential equation to take this into account. Sketch the new graph of versus . Determine the new equilibrium solutions and decide whether they are stable or unstable.    Given the situation in part (b), give a description of the long-term behavior of the fish population.    Suppose that fishermen remove thousand fish per month. How is the differential equation modified?    What is the largest number of fish that can be removed per month without eliminating the fish population? If fish are removed at this maximum rate, what is the eventual population of fish?          A graph of against is given in blue in the figure below. The equilibrium solutions are (unstable) and (stable).        ; the equilibrium at is unstable; the equilibrium at is stable.    If , then the fish population will die out. If , then the fish population will approach thousand fish.     ; equilibrium solutions .     fish; harvesting at that rate will maintain the number of fish we start with, provided it's at least .          A graph of against is given in blue in the figure below. This graph and the differential equation show that the equilibrium solutions occur when , or when or . Since for slightly less than 0, the solutions with decrease away from the equilibrium . Also, for slightly larger than 0 means that the solutions for increase away from the equilibrium . So is an unstable equilibrium. If is slightly less than we see that , so these solutions increase toward the equilibrium . When is slightly larger than , we have and so these solutions decrease toward the equilibrium . We conclude that the equilibrium solution is stable.       Since will decrease by thousand fish every month through harvesting, the new differential equation is . A sketch of against is given in green in the figure in (a). The equilibrium solutions now occur when . This happens when . The quadratic formula tells us that the solutions to this quadratic equation are . The same argument as in (a) shows that the equilibrium at is unstable and the equilibrium at is stable.    Assuming that there are fish in the lake, so that , there are two cases to consider. If , then and the fish population will die out. If , then and the fish population will increase and approach a stable equilibrium population of thousand fish. If , then and the fish population will decrease and approach a stable equilibrium population of thousand fish.    In this case the differential equation becomes . We will have equilibrium solutions when or when .    We need a positive stable equilibrium. This will will happen as long as or when . So the maximum harvest is fish. Note that is negative, so we need to start with more than fish. Since has a maximum value of , the fish will reproduce quickly enough so that harvesting fish will maintain a stable population.      "
