  "body": "  We have seen that the error in approximating the solution to an initial value problem is proportional to . That is, if is the Euler's method approximation to the solution to an initial value problem at , then for some constant of proportionality .  In this problem, we will see how to use this fact to improve our estimates, using an idea called accelerated convergence .   We will create a new approximation by assuming the error is exactly proportional to , according to the formula . Using our earlier results from the initial value problem and with and , we have . This is a system of two linear equations in the unknowns and . Solve this system to find a new approximation for . (You may remember that the exact value is .)    Use the other data, and to do similar work as in (a) to obtain another approximation. Which gives the better approximation? Why do you think this is?    Let's now study the initial value problem . Approximate by applying Euler's method to find approximations and . Now use the idea of accelerated convergence to obtain a better approximation. (For the sake of comparison, you want to note that the actual value is .)           ; .     ; .     and .          To solve the linear system we subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate . Doing so, we find that so that and thus . Substituting this value into the first equation in the system, it follows that so .    Recalling that and , it follows that this data generates the similar linear system given by . Solving this system yields and . Knowing that the true value of is , we see that the approximation here in (b) is much better. That is expected because we are already working with better approximations that come from using and , which have been twice halved from the smaller value in (a).    To compute and , we first compute the estimates to using and . Doing so and making similar assumptions about the error as we did in (a) and (b), we find that for , and for , , and therefore we consider the system . Solving the system, it follows that and , which is much closer to the actual value of .      "
