  "body": "  During the first few years of life, the rate at which a baby gains weight is proportional to the reciprocal of its weight.   Express this fact as a differential equation.    Suppose that a baby weighs 8 pounds at birth and 9 pounds one month later. How much will he weigh at one year?    Do you think this is a realistic model for a long time?           .     ; pounds.    The model is unrealistic.          Since the baby's weight, (in pounds at month ), grows at a rate proportional to its reciprocal, we have .    Given that and , we can both solve the IVP and determine . To start, we separate variables and solve the differential equation by writing and integrating to get . It follows that and therefore (where and we take the positive square root because we know ). Applying the initial condition , we find that , so and . To find , we use the additional information that , so which implies that and thus . Thus, . At one year, according to the model the baby weighs pounds.    While the function grows without bound as increases, the model is unrealistic more because the function initially doesn't grow fast enough. For example, the model predicts that at age , pounds, and at age , pounds. A realistic model would need to grow much more quickly early in life and then level off around age .      "
