  "body": "  Using some recent population data for planet Earth, we want to model the growth of the population over time.   Some recent population data (in billions) for planet Earth.    Year  1998  1999  2000  2001  2002  2005  2006  2007  2008  2009  2010    Pop                Our first model will be based on the following assumption:    The rate of change of the population is proportional to the population.    On the face of it, this seems pretty reasonable. When there is a relatively small number of people, there will be fewer births and deaths so the rate of change will be small. When there is a larger number of people, there will be more births and deaths so we expect a larger rate of change. If is the population years after the year 2000, we may express this assumption as where is a constant of proportionality.     Use the data in the table to estimate the derivative using a central difference. Assume that corresponds to the year 2000.    Small hints for each of the prompts above.    .    Using the data in the table, .      What is the population ?     .    Since corresponds to the year , .      Use your results from (a) and (b) to estimate the constant of proportionality in the differential equation.     .    Using and the preceding values at , we have , so .      Now that we know the value of , we have the initial value problem . Find the solution to this initial value problem.     .    The solution for the initial value problem is .      What does your solution predict for the population in the year 2010? Is this close to the actual population given in the table?     .    The year 2010 corresponds to . So . The model predicts that the population in 2010 will be about 6.888 billion.      When does your solution predict that the population will reach 12 billion?     , or in the year 2056.    The population will be 12 billion when . So we solve the equation for . We see that , so . The population will reach 12 billion during the year 2056.      What does your solution predict for the population in the year 2500?     billion.    The year 2500 corresponds to . So . The model predicts that the population in 2500 will be about 3012 billion or about about 3 012 300 000 000. This is rather unreasonably large.    "
