  "number": "7.6.5.6",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The logistic equation may be used to model how a rumor spreads through a group of people. Suppose that is the fraction of people that have heard the rumor on day . The equation describes how changes. Suppose initially that one-tenth of the people have heard the rumor; that is, .   What happens to after a very long time?    Determine a formula for the function .    At what time is changing most rapidly?    How long does it take before 80% of the people have heard the rumor?           as provided .     .     days.     days.          The equilibrium solutions for this logistic equation are and . Since is the carrying capacity and thus a stable equilibrium, as provided .    We know that the general solution to the logistic equation is . In the context of this problem, we know that , , and and therefore the solution is .    We know that is changing most rapidly when its derivative, , is largest. Recalling the original differential equation, we know that which shows that is a quadratic function of . That quadratic function has zeros at and , so the vertex of this concave down quadratic function lies at , and at this value of the rate of change of is largest. Thus, we solve the equation to find the time at which the population is increasing fastest. We see that so and . It follows that days.    Since tells us the percentage of people who've heard the rumor, we need to solve the equation . Thus we have so and . It follows that days.      "
