  "number": "7.6.5.8",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Suppose that the population of a species of fish is controlled by the logistic equation , where is measured in thousands of fish and is measured in years.   What is the carrying capacity of this population?    Suppose that a long time has passed and that the fish population is stable at the carrying capacity. At this time, humans begin harvesting 20% of the fish every year. Modify the differential equation by adding a term to incorporate the harvesting of fish.    What is the new carrying capacity?    What will the fish population be one year after the harvesting begins?    How long will it take for the population to be within 10% of the carrying capacity?          10000 fish.     .     fish.     thousand fish.     years.          From the form of the given logistic equation, we can see the carrying capacity is 10000 fish.    In this situation, the initial condition is , and by removing % of the population per year, we subtract from the rate that defines the differential equation, thus finding .    Expanding and rewriting the differential equation in (b), we see that . This, too, is a logistic equation, with carrying capacity fish.    We can solve this logistic equation in the usual way (keeping in mind the initial condition , and noting that the carrying capacity is ): After one year, the population of fish will thus be thousand fish.    To be within 10% of the carrying capacity, we need . Since the initial condition is larger than the carrying capacity, we want to know when . Thus we solve the equation , which implies . Thus, , so years.      "
