  "body": "  The fact that we can differentiate or integrate a Taylor series reveals other important ways we can think about functions such as .    Perhaps the most important property of the function is that ; that is, the function is its own derivative. Suppose that we didn't yet know the coefficients of the Taylor series expansion for , so we just said . Let in Equation ; what does this tell us about the value of ?    Take the derivative of both sides of Equation and call your resulting equation for  Equation 2 . Why do Equation and Equation 2 together tell us that ? Combine this observation with your conclusion in (b) and note that you now know the numerical value of both and .    Why do Equation and Equation 2 together tell us that ?    Continue reasoning similarly to find the value of , , and . What do you observe?          .    Both series expanstions for have as their constant term, and thus .    By equating the coefficients of the linear terms and , it follows .     , , and ; so in general, .        Letting in shows that , so .    Differentiating both sides of Equation , since we find . By equating the constant terms in Equation and in our most recent equation (Equation 2), we see that . Having earlier established that , we now also know .    If we equate the coefficients of the linear terms and in Equation and Equation 2, we see that , so .    Equating coefficients of like terms in Equation and Equation 2, we find that , , and . This implies that , , and . Thus we observe that , which is exactly what we'd expect for the Taylor series of .     "
